<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">William of Jumièges</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1000–after 1070</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">William of Jumièges was a monk of the Norman abbey of Jumièges and the author of the Gesta Normannorum ducum, a history of the dukes of Normandy. Building on the work of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dudo-of-saint-quentin">Dudo of Saint-Quentin</a>, he continued the story to the conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and presented it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-the-conqueror">William the Conqueror</a> around 1070. His work was later extended by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orderic-vitalis">Orderic Vitalis</a> and Robert of Torigni.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/william-of-jumieges.jpg" alt="William of Jumièges" width="277" height="369" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: AnonymousUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jumieges.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
